    "## Tests\n",
    "\n",
    "For each pair, the monthly loss differential is $d_t = L^{XGB}_t - L^{EN}_t$ (negative favours XGBoost). The test statistic is `gain_t = -hac_t(d)`, so **`gain_t > 0` favours XGBoost**. The pooled row averages $d_t$ across the five ETFs each month before testing. The ETF rows test each ETF's own series. The p-value is one-sided, $H_1$: XGBoost beats EN.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both losses use DM, because the pairs are non-nested. A significant result means XGBoost is better. An insignificant result means the two cannot be told apart, not that they are equally good."
=======
    "## 4. Loss functions\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $y_t=\\log RV_t$ and $\\hat y_t$ the forecast of log RV (`fc_log`). Let $RV_t$ be the realised variance and $F_t$ the level forecast (`fc_level`).\n",
    "\n",
    "**MSE on log RV**\n",
    "\n",
    "$$L^{MSE}_t=(y_t-\\hat y_t)^2$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**QLIKE on RV levels** (normalised form, if `QLIKE_NORMALISED=True`)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$L^{QLIKE}_t=\\frac{RV_t}{F_t}-\\ln\\!\\frac{RV_t}{F_t}-1$$\n",
    "\n",
    "QLIKE is asymmetric: it penalises **under-prediction** of volatility more than over-prediction. This is why it is a useful complement to MSE, which treats both directions equally. Both losses are \"lower is better\".\n",
    "\n",
    "**Loss ratio.** I report $\\text{MSE}_{XGB}/\\text{MSE}_{EN}$ and $\\text{QLIKE}_{XGB}/\\text{QLIKE}_{EN}$. A ratio **below 1 means XGBoost has the lower average loss**, i.e. non-linearity helped. A ratio above 1 means EN is better.\n",
    "\n",
    "> `fc_level` is taken as given from the files. For both models it equals $\\exp(\\hat y_t+\\sigma^2_t/2)$, the log-normal retransformation using the `sigma2` column. I checked this on the data. If your group intended a different level forecast (e.g. $\\exp(\\hat y_t)$), QLIKE results will change."
