   "source": [
    "## 6. The Diebold–Mariano test: what it does and why it is valid here\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea.** For each month compute the loss differential $d_t=L^{EN}_t-L^{XGB}_t$. Under the null of equal predictive accuracy, $E[d_t]=0$. The DM statistic is the standardised mean of $d_t$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$DM=\\frac{\\bar d}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(\\bar d)}},\\qquad \\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(\\bar d)=\\frac{1}{T}\\Big(\\hat\\gamma_0+2\\sum_{k=1}^{h-1}\\hat\\gamma_k\\Big)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\hat\\gamma_k$ is the sample autocovariance of $d_t$ at lag $k$.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Horizon.** With $h=1$ (one month ahead) the sum is empty. One-step-ahead errors are not expected to be serially correlated by construction, so no HAC lags are added. For $h>1$ the code includes the $h-1$ autocovariances (rectangular kernel).\n",
    "* **Small-sample correction (`HLN_CORRECTION=True`).** The statistic is multiplied by $\\sqrt{\\frac{T+1-2h+h(h-1)/T}{T}}$ (Harvey, Leybourne & Newbold, 1997) and compared with a Student-$t_{T-1}$ distribution instead of the normal. This is the standard fix when $T$ is only a couple of hundred months.\n",
    "* **Sign convention.** $d_t>0$ means XGB had the lower loss. I report:\n",
    "  * a **two-sided** p-value ($H_1$: the models differ), and\n",
    "  * a **one-sided** p-value ($H_1$: *XGB is better than EN*). This is the direct answer to *\"does non-linearity add anything?\"*.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why DM and not Clark–West here.** DM's non-standard distribution problem arises when comparing **nested** models, where the larger model collapses to the smaller one under the null. EN and XGB are **different model classes** on the *same inputs*, so neither is a special case of the other. The pair is non-nested and DM with standard critical values is appropriate.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Per-ETF vs pooled.** Each ETF is tested separately ($T$ = number of out-of-sample months). If `POOL_ETFS=True`, I also test the **cross-ETF mean** of $d_t$ for each month. Averaging within a month first keeps the test statistic valid despite the strong correlation between ETF losses in the same month."
   ]
  },
